  "details": "Establish a VRChat Loudness Standard (based on LUFS) for sound design and voice volume balancing.\n\nGoal: To end the status quo of having to constantly messing with sliders all the time on different people / different avatars / different worlds.\n\n1. Publish some sort of VRChat audio loudness specification. Adopt EBU R 128 (or any other standard) to become the VRChat platform standard. Such as specifying an expected LUFS for player voice, avatar sounds, and world sounds. Similar to how other platforms have their own LUFS targets (e.g. Youtube has a -14 LUFS standard). This will provide a precise target (or loudness ceiling) for avatar/world designers to master their sounds, and how players should set their mic input volume.\n\n2. Align VRchat internal sound level processing to above.\n\n3. Add automatic normalization to avatar sounds and world sounds as a \"comfort and safety\" toggle. This can be done by measuring and normalizing their sound data when they are loaded. (This gives a \"backwards compatibility\" to avatar and worlds created prior to this standard, or creators who does not follow the standard. This works independently from a compressor, which is only a safety net and not a method of loudness balancing.)\n\n4. Add a \"measure my microphone levels/LUFS\" wizard next to the mic volume settings, have the player read out a paragraph (mimicking an average conversation) and measure the LUFS, then recommend (or adjust) their mic input volume to a standard-conforming level.",
